48)\n  35. pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584212)\n  36. Chemical origin of life articles within Nature Chemistry (https://www.nature.com/subjects/origin-of-life/nchem)\n  37. Cyanide as a primordial reductant enables a protometabolic reductive glyoxylate pathway (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-021-00878-w)\n  38. The Krishnamurthy Lab (https://www.scripps.edu/krishnamurthy/publications.html)\n  39. Conversion of pyridoxal to pyridoxamine with NH 3 and H 2 on nickel generates a protometabolic nitrogen shuttle under serpentinizing conditions - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7617359/)\n  40. Review Article The Production of Methane, Hydrogen, and Organic (https://www.cbd.int/doc/c/fb08/ead7/9cd226d067d65d7b01487ab7/hydrothermal-vents-konn-et-al-2015-en.pdf)\n  41. alpha-Hydroxy and alpha-amino acids under possible Hadean, volcanic origin-of-life conditions - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17068257/)\n  42. www.sciencemag.org SCIENCE VOL 315 16 FEBRUARY 2007 937 (https://hazen.carnegiescience.edu/sites/hazen.gl.ciw.edu/files/3.pdf)\n  43. Geoelectrochemical CO production: Implications for the autotrophic origin of life (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aao7265)\n  44. The stability of amino acids at submarine hydrothermal vent temperatures (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01581577)\n  45. The stability of amino acids at submarine hydrothermal vent temperatures - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11536665/)\n  46. The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2)\n  47. Abiotic synthesis of amino acids in the recesses of the oceanic lithosphere - Nature (https://nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0684-z)\n  48. Shock wave synthesis of amino acids from solutions of ammonium formate and ammonium bicarbonate - Suzuki - 2015 - Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems - Wiley Online Library (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2015GC005783)\n  49. Meteorite impacts on the early Earth and origin of life (https://www2.jpgu.org/meeting/2012/session/PDF/M-IS01/MIS01-19_e.pdf)\n  50. Synthesis of glycine-containing complexes in impacts of comets on early Earth (https://www.nature.com/articles/nchem.827)\n  51. Towards Prebiotic Chemistry on Titan: Impact experiments on organic haze particles (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.00029)\n  52. Survivability and reactivity of glycine and alanine in early oceans: effects of meteorite impacts (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10867-015-9400-5)\n  53. Detection of Glycine in Comet 67P/ Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://rosetta.jpl.nasa.gov/rosetta-science-blog/detection-glycine-comet-67p/churyumov-gerasimenko)\n  54. Glycine and dark chemistry in a lab as cold as space - COSMOS Education (https://education.cosmosmagazine.com/dark-chemistry-in-a-lab-as-cold-as-space/)\n  55. Cometary glycine detected in samples returned by Stardust - Elsila - 2009 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1945-5100.2009.tb01224.x)\n  56. Prebiotic chemicals\u2014amino acid and phosphorus\u2014in the coma of comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.1600285)\n  57. Distributed glycine in comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2019/10/aa35018-19/aa35018-19.html)\n  58. Naraoka 2023.pdf - Sciences and Exploration Directorate (https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Naraoka%202023.pdf)\n  59. Soluble organic molecules in samples of the carbonaceous asteroid (162173) Ryugu (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abn9033)\n  60. Organic matter from Bennu is rich in ammonia and nitrogen \u2014 AskAstrobio (https://askastrobio.org/articles/organic-matter-from-bennu)\n  61. Life's Building Blocks Found in Bennu Samples - Eos.org (https://eos.org/articles/lifes-building-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.77\n- Feedback: The network is connected, uses consistent molecule IDs, and its overall route logic matches the literature well by centering aminoacetonitrile and glyoxylate or glycine-imine hubs while avoiding prolonged residence above about 200 C. However, it is not yet a fully valid synthesis network: rxn_008, rxn_010, and rxn_011 are ground-truth conservation violations; rxn_004 should be mol_004 + 2 mol_006 -> mol_012 + mol_003; and rxn_006 should be mol_008 + 2 mol_006 -> mol_012 + mol_003 + mol_007. The strongest branch is P10, with P1 and P2 also credible once stoichiometry is corrected, but the HCN oligomer shortcut must be replaced by a multistep mass-balanced module and the Fe and green-rust reductions need explicit hydrogen or electron bookkeeping or oxidized mineral products. Environmental labeling also needs refinement because several mineral or aqueous steps are tagged only as Biochemical, and inter-environment transfers are not always justified explicitly. Literature alignment is good at the route level, though explicit per-reaction citation mapping, glycolate side branches, and the known constraints on concentrated HCN and glyoxylate sourcing would improve fidelity. As submitted, the network is not approved.\n- Issues: ['The Bucherer-Bergs or hydantoin concept is literature-supported and correctly connects aminoacetonitrile to glycine through CO2 capture. The weakness is that rxn_006 needs 2 H2O for conservation, and the pathway compresses carbamate formation, cyclization, and hydrolysis into oversimplified steps with no explicit formation and hydrolysis conditions. Environmental fit would improve if hydantoin formation and hydrolysis were assigned to defined aqueous conditions rather than Biochemical.', 'This branch is chemically sensible as a glycolonitrile entry into the hydantoin route and is consistent with the network hub logic. As in P3, rxn_006 needs 2 H2O, and the route should state the aqueous CO2 or bicarbonate trapping and later hydrolysis conditions more explicitly. Literature alignment is good, but the route would be stronger if glycolate competition were encoded.', 'Moderate hydrothermal Strecker synthesis followed by cooler hydrolysis is consistent with published hydrothermal Strecker work and with the literature warning to avoid long exposure above about 200 C. The main correction is stoichiometric: rxn_004 needs 2 H2O. Environmental consistency is decent only if the post-vent quench or handoff to a cooler aqueous phase is made explicit.', 'Hydrothermal formation of aminoacetonitrile followed by post-vent CO2 trapping and hydantoin hydrolysis is a coherent attempt to respect glycine thermolability. However, rxn_006 needs 2 H2O, and the pathway should make the quench into a cooler CO2-rich aqueous environment explicit rather than relying on the vague Biochemical label.', 'The literature does support glycine release from HCN oligomer hydrolysis, but not as the one-step reaction encoded here. rxn_008 is a ground-truth conservation violation: one HCN plus one water cannot produce two-carbon glycine, and the balance check indicates no simple small-species fix. This branch must be replaced by a multistep, mass-balanced oligomerization and hydrolysis subnetwork.', 'The glyoxylate to glycine imine to glycine logic matches the literature, and Fe-mediated reduction is experimentally supported. But rxn_010 as written is unbalanced and missing a hydrogen or electron source; the balance check specifically indicates that H2 is missing if a simplified bookkeeping step is intended. A stronger representation would add explicit Fe redox bookkeeping or an explicit reductant and relabel the environment as an Fe-mineral aqueous interface rather than Biochemical.', 'The green-rust route is literature-aligned conceptually, especially for pH 7 to 10 mineral interfaces, but the current final step is not a valid reaction. rxn_011 is unbalanced and needs explicit H2 or equivalent hydrogen and electron bookkeeping with oxidized mineral products. Environmental naming should also be revised to a mineral-interface setting.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network centers the two most experimentally supported prebiotic glycine hubs identified in the literature: aminoacetonitrile from HCN-formaldehyde-ammonia chemistry and glycine imine from glyoxylate-ammonia chemistry. Surface branches generate aminoacetonitrile either stepwise through glycolonitrile or directly by Strecker chemistry, then convert it to glycine by aqueous hydrolysis or by a CO2-trapped hydantoin detour. A parallel mineral-interface branch condenses glyoxylate with ammonia and reduces the imine on Fe- or Ni-bearing surfaces, while a glycolate branch captures the known hydroxy-acid competition. Hydrothermal chemistry is limited to moderate Strecker conditions with rapid quench, because glycine is thermolabile at prolonged higher temperatures. Concentrated HCN and prebiotic glyoxylate supply remain the principal upstream bottlenecks.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid / glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-iminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolic acid / glycolate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h3H,1H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Uncatalyzed cyanohydrin formation of glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic evaporitic or tidal-flat pool; concentration by evaporation or freezing can raise local HCN and formaldehyde.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin formation by nucleophilic addition of cyanide to formaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is the direct cyanohydrin of formaldehyde and HCN and a recognized branch point toward amino acid synthesis in cyanide-based prebiotic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The chemistry itself is straightforward and uncatalyzed in water; the real uncertainty is geochemical, namely whether evaporation, freezing, or other local concentration mechanisms could maintain enough HCN and formaldehyde (Johnson et al., 2008; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemical access to glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation, phosphate-rich stream or pool, HS- present in the broader cyanosulfidic network, anoxic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin formation embedded in a UV-driven Cu photoredox cyanosulfidic reaction network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. showed that glycolonitrile participates in UV-driven Cu photoredox cyanosulfidic networks that also access amino acid precursors (Patel et al., 2015). This route is experimentally grounded, but the surface scenario is contested because it requires sequential cyanide-rich, sulfide-bearing, UV-irradiated settings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline pH about 8-9; elevated ammonia favors aminonitrile over hydroxy-acid production.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonolysis of the cyanohydrin followed by dehydration to the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Exposure of glycolonitrile to ammonia gives aminoacetonitrile, the key Strecker hub intermediate; higher NH3 and mildly alkaline conditions favor this aminonitrile branch over hydroxy-acid production (Patel et al., 2015). This step is therefore chemically strong but still inherits the general cyanide concentration problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface aqueous Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline pool or discharge trap; local cyanide concentration by evaporation, freezing, or reducing plumes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia form an imine that is trapped by cyanide to give the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall aqueous Strecker entry from formaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN to aminoacetonitrile is one of the best supported glycine routes and underlies Miller-type amino acid production (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011). The mechanism is not seriously disputed; the debated issue is whether early Earth could sustain sufficiently reducing, HCN-rich local surface settings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyrite\", \"pyrrhotite\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Redox-buffering mineral assemblage\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2/H2 gas phase, rapid quench into cooler water required; minerals act mainly as buffer rather than proven direct catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker condensation under redox-buffered aqueous conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker chemistry has experimental support at about 150 C and 10 bar under CO2/H2 with a pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite buffer, as reported by Hennet et al. and summarized by Andersson and Holm, 2000. This branch is kept only at moderate hydrothermal conditions and assumes rapid quench, because amino acids are progressively destroyed at higher or prolonged vent temperatures (Andersson and Holm, 2000; Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool prebiotic pool or aqueous alteration setting; net stoichiometry consumes 2 H2O.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis through the amide to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile is the accepted terminal step of Strecker glycine synthesis; this net node represents the overall conversion with two water equivalents consumed and ammonia released. The step requires no catalyst, though acid-base cycling and prolonged aqueous residence accelerate it, and it is implicit in both classic Miller reanalyses and hydrothermal Strecker product formation (Johnson et al., 2008; Andersson and Holm, 2000).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 capture and cyclization of aminoacetonitrile to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2- or bicarbonate-rich pool; carbamylation followed by cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO2 trapping as a carbamate followed by intramolecular cyclization to hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"CO2 can trap an aminonitrile as a carbamylated intermediate that cyclizes to hydantoin, the Bucherer-Bergs logic. Pulletikurti et al., 2022 demonstrated this framework for prebiotic amino acid synthesis, and Kaur et al., 2024 highlight hydantoin formation as an important bridge between cyanide chemistry and amino acid release. The route is promising but remains cyanide-dependent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous hydrolysis of hydantoin to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous hydrolysis in a concentrated pool; net stoichiometry consumes 2 H2O.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring opening, hydrolysis, and decarboxylative release of glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoins hydrolyze to amino acids under warm aqueous conditions. This mass-balanced overall step compresses ring opening, hydrolysis, and decarboxylation into a single node and is consistent with both hydantoin release from HCN-derived material and modern Bucherer-Bergs hydrolyses (Ferris et al., 1974; Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-ammonia condensation to glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to moderate\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prebiotic pool or mineral interface, pH 8-10, reversible hemiaminal-imine equilibrium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia addition to glyoxylate followed by dehydration to the imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Paczelt et al., 2023 directly identified glycine imine from glyoxylate-ammonia chemistry, and Mayer and Moran, 2022 showed that glyoxylate is exceptionally reactive toward reductive amination relative to other biological keto acids. The chemistry of this condensation is therefore strong; the unresolved issue is not the step itself but obtaining enough glyoxylate prebiotically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-mineral reduction of glycine imine to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+/Fe0\", \"green rust / mixed-valence iron hydroxides\", \"ferroan brucite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to a few bar local H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-bearing mineral interface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH about 7-10 at an anoxic mineral interface; mineral Fe(II)/Fe(0) or geoelectrochemical reduction can supply electrons; H2 is shown as net reductant.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-assisted hydrogen and electron transfer reduces the imine C=N bond to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fe-bearing systems repeatedly support glycine formation from glyoxylate-derived imines, including Fe2+/Fe0 promoted networks, mixed-valence iron hydroxides or green rust, electroreduced FeS to Fe0 interfaces, and ferroan brucite (Muchowska et al., 2019; Barge et al., 2020; Kitadai et al., 2019; Chimiak et al., 2024). Many experiments use mineral Fe rather than free H2 as the actual electron donor, so H2 is included here as net reducing-equivalent bookkeeping to keep the node atom-balanced. Selectivity against glycolate is incomplete, and Chimiak et al. also found strong mineral binding of product in some brucite systems.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni or native-metal hydrogenation of glycine imine to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni-bearing metal/alloy\", \"ground meteoritic metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 or local equivalent\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/native-metal surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8; mineral interface setting; synthetic supported Ni catalysts serve as laboratory analogs for natural native-metal surfaces.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Heterogeneous hydrogenation of the imine on a metal surface.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al., 2024 showed that H2 over Ni catalysts or ground iron meteorites converts glyoxylate-derived intermediates to glycine under mild conditions and at unusually low ammonium concentrations. This is one of the strongest low-temperature reductive amination results, but the best supported Ni catalysts are synthetic supports, so translation to natural native-metal or awaruite-like surfaces remains partly inferential.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing reduction of glyoxylate to glycolate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe-bearing minerals\", \"Ni-bearing metals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1-5 bar H2 or local equivalent\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe/Ni mineral surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Competing hydroxy-acid branch favored when ammonia capture is slow or low.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct carbonyl reduction to the alpha-hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct reduction of glyoxylate to glycolate is the main hydroxy-acid competitor to glycine in the glyoxylate branch and should be explicit in the network. Both Fe-hydroxide and Ni/H2 systems produce substantial glycolate, often approaching parity with glycine when NH3 capture is not strongly favored (Barge et al., 2020; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Uncatalyzed cyanohydrin to glycine\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and HCN first give glycolonitrile in a concentrated surface pool; ammonia then converts it to aminoacetonitrile, which hydrolyzes in a cooler aqueous pool to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Uncatalyzed cyanohydrin through hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"The same surface glycolonitrile entry is diverted through aminoacetonitrile capture by CO2 to hydantoin before warm hydrolysis releases glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic glycolonitrile hydrolysis route\",\n      \"description\": \"UV and Cu photoredox surface chemistry generates glycolonitrile, followed by aminolysis to aminoacetonitrile and aqueous hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic glycolonitrile hydantoin route\",\n      \"description\": \"A cyanosulfidic surface setting furnishes glycolonitrile, which is aminated to aminoacetonitrile and then trapped as hydantoin before hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN, formaldehyde, and ammonia condense directly to aminoacetonitrile in a surface pool, followed by aqueous hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface direct Strecker with hydantoin detour\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface Strecker chemistry produces aminoacetonitrile, which is trapped by CO2 as hydantoin and then hydrolyzed to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker plus quench hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Aminoacetonitrile forms in a redox-buffered moderate hydrothermal setting and is then hydrolyzed after rapid quench into a cooler aqueous pool.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker plus hydantoin detour\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal aminoacetonitrile is transferred after quench into CO2-rich water, trapped as hydantoin, and then hydrolyzed to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate reductive amination on Fe minerals\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate condenses with ammonia to glycine imine, which is reduced on Fe-bearing mineral interfaces such as Fe2+/Fe0 systems, green rust, or brucite-like precipitates.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate reductive amination on Ni or native metal\",\n      \"description\": \"The same glycine imine intermediate is hydrogenated on Ni-bearing or meteoritic metal surfaces under mild H2-rich conditions to yield glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "The network centers the two most experimentally supported prebiotic glycine hubs identified in the literature: aminoacetonitrile from HCN-formaldehyde-ammonia chemistry and glycine imine from glyoxylate-ammonia chemistry. Surface branches generate aminoacetonitrile either stepwise through glycolonitrile or directly by Strecker chemistry, then convert it to glycine by aqueous hydrolysis or by a CO2-trapped hydantoin detour. A parallel mineral-interface branch condenses glyoxylate with ammonia and reduces the imine on Fe- or Ni-bearing surfaces, while a glycolate branch captures the known hydroxy-acid competition. Hydrothermal chemistry is limited to moderate Strecker conditions with rapid quench, because glycine is thermolabile at prolonged higher temperatures. Concentrated HCN and prebiotic glyoxylate supply remain the principal upstream bottlenecks.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid / glyoxylate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H3NO2", "iupac_name": "2-iminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine imine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetic acid", "common_name": "glycolic acid / glycolate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h3H,1H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Uncatalyzed cyanohydrin formation of glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic evaporitic or tidal-flat pool; concentration by evaporation or freezing can raise local HCN and formaldehyde."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin formation by nucleophilic addition of cyanide to formaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile is the direct cyanohydrin of formaldehyde and HCN and a recognized branch point toward amino acid synthesis in cyanide-based prebiotic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The chemistry itself is straightforward and uncatalyzed in water; the real uncertainty is geochemical, namely whether evaporation, freezing, or other local concentration mechanisms could maintain enough HCN and formaldehyde (Johnson et al., 2008; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanosulfidic photochemical access to glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation, phosphate-rich stream or pool, HS- present in the broader cyanosulfidic network, anoxic."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin formation embedded in a UV-driven Cu photoredox cyanosulfidic reaction network.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. showed that glycolonitrile participates in UV-driven Cu photoredox cyanosulfidic networks that also access amino acid precursors (Patel et al., 2015). This route is experimentally grounded, but the surface scenario is contested because it requires sequential cyanide-rich, sulfide-bearing, UV-irradiated settings."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Amination of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline pH about 8-9; elevated ammonia favors aminonitrile over hydroxy-acid production."}, "mechanism": "Ammonolysis of the cyanohydrin followed by dehydration to the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Exposure of glycolonitrile to ammonia gives aminoacetonitrile, the key Strecker hub intermediate; higher NH3 and mildly alkaline conditions favor this aminonitrile branch over hydroxy-acid production (Patel et al., 2015). This step is therefore chemically strong but still inherits the general cyanide concentration problem."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Surface aqueous Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline pool or discharge trap; local cyanide concentration by evaporation, freezing, or reducing plumes."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde and ammonia form an imine that is trapped by cyanide to give the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "The overall aqueous Strecker entry from formaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN to aminoacetonitrile is one of the best supported glycine routes and underlies Miller-type amino acid production (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011). The mechanism is not seriously disputed; the debated issue is whether early Earth could sustain sufficiently reducing, HCN-rich local surface settings."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["pyrite", "pyrrhotite", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temp