s.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2015GC005783)\n  49. Meteorite impacts on the early Earth and origin of life (https://www2.jpgu.org/meeting/2012/session/PDF/M-IS01/MIS01-19_e.pdf)\n  50. Synthesis of glycine-containing complexes in impacts of comets on early Earth (https://www.nature.com/articles/nchem.827)\n  51. Towards Prebiotic Chemistry on Titan: Impact experiments on organic haze particles (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.00029)\n  52. Survivability and reactivity of glycine and alanine in early oceans: effects of meteorite impacts (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10867-015-9400-5)\n  53. Detection of Glycine in Comet 67P/ Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://rosetta.jpl.nasa.gov/rosetta-science-blog/detection-glycine-comet-67p/churyumov-gerasimenko)\n  54. Glycine and dark chemistry in a lab as cold as space - COSMOS Education (https://education.cosmosmagazine.com/dark-chemistry-in-a-lab-as-cold-as-space/)\n  55. Cometary glycine detected in samples returned by Stardust - Elsila - 2009 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1945-5100.2009.tb01224.x)\n  56. Prebiotic chemicals\u2014amino acid and phosphorus\u2014in the coma of comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.1600285)\n  57. Distributed glycine in comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2019/10/aa35018-19/aa35018-19.html)\n  58. Naraoka 2023.pdf - Sciences and Exploration Directorate (https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Naraoka%202023.pdf)\n  59. Soluble organic molecules in samples of the carbonaceous asteroid (162173) Ryugu (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abn9033)\n  60. Organic matter from Bennu is rich in ammonia and nitrogen \u2014 AskAstrobio (https://askastrobio.org/articles/organic-matter-from-bennu)\n  61. Life's Building Blocks Found in Bennu Samples - Eos.org (https://eos.org/articles/lifes-building-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.77\n- Feedback: The network is connected, uses consistent molecule IDs, and its overall route logic matches the literature well by centering aminoacetonitrile and glyoxylate or glycine-imine hubs while avoiding prolonged residence above about 200 C. However, it is not yet a fully valid synthesis network: rxn_008, rxn_010, and rxn_011 are ground-truth conservation violations; rxn_004 should be mol_004 + 2 mol_006 -> mol_012 + mol_003; and rxn_006 should be mol_008 + 2 mol_006 -> mol_012 + mol_003 + mol_007. The strongest branch is P10, with P1 and P2 also credible once stoichiometry is corrected, but the HCN oligomer shortcut must be replaced by a multistep mass-balanced module and the Fe and green-rust reductions need explicit hydrogen or electron bookkeeping or oxidized mineral products. Environmental labeling also needs refinement because several mineral or aqueous steps are tagged only as Biochemical, and inter-environment transfers are not always justified explicitly. Literature alignment is good at the route level, though explicit per-reaction citation mapping, glycolate side branches, and the known constraints on concentrated HCN and glyoxylate sourcing would improve fidelity. As submitted, the network is not approved.\n- Issues: ['The Bucherer-Bergs or hydantoin concept is literature-supported and correctly connects aminoacetonitrile to glycine through CO2 capture. The weakness is that rxn_006 needs 2 H2O for conservation, and the pathway compresses carbamate formation, cyclization, and hydrolysis into oversimplified steps with no explicit formation and hydrolysis conditions. Environmental fit would improve if hydantoin formation and hydrolysis were assigned to defined aqueous conditions rather than Biochemical.', 'This branch is chemically sensible as a glycolonitrile entry into the hydantoin route and is consistent with the network hub logic. As in P3, rxn_006 needs 2 H2O, and the route should state the aqueous CO2 or bicarbonate trapping and later hydrolysis conditions more explicitly. Literature alignment is good, but the route would be stronger if glycolate competition were encoded.', 'Moderate hydrothermal Strecker synthesis followed by cooler hydrolysis is consistent with published hydrothermal Strecker work and with the literature warning to avoid long exposure above about 200 C. The main correction is stoichiometric: rxn_004 needs 2 H2O. Environmental consistency is decent only if the post-vent quench or handoff to a cooler aqueous phase is made explicit.', 'Hydrothermal formation of aminoacetonitrile followed by post-vent CO2 trapping and hydantoin hydrolysis is a coherent attempt to respect glycine thermolability. However, rxn_006 needs 2 H2O, and the pathway should make the quench into a cooler CO2-rich aqueous environment explicit rather than relying on the vague Biochemical label.', 'The literature does support glycine release from HCN oligomer hydrolysis, but not as the one-step reaction encoded here. rxn_008 is a ground-truth conservation violation: one HCN plus one water cannot produce two-carbon glycine, and the balance check indicates no simple small-species fix. This branch must be replaced by a multistep, mass-balanced oligomerization and hydrolysis subnetwork.', 'The glyoxylate to glycine imine to glycine logic matches the literature, and Fe-mediated reduction is experimentally supported. But rxn_010 as written is unbalanced and missing a hydrogen or electron source; the balance check specifically indicates that H2 is missing if a simplified bookkeeping step is intended. A stronger representation would add explicit Fe redox bookkeeping or an explicit reductant and relabel the environment as an Fe-mineral aqueous interface rather than Biochemical.', 'The green-rust route is literature-aligned conceptually, especially for pH 7 to 10 mineral interfaces, but the current final step is not a valid reaction. rxn_011 is unbalanced and needs explicit H2 or equivalent hydrogen and electron bookkeeping with oxidized mineral products. Environmental naming should also be revised to a mineral-interface setting.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.84\n- Feedback: Connected and literature-aware network. It has enough molecule and reaction nodes, no isolated subgraphs, and the deterministic balance check reports 0 unbalanced reactions. The two balanceable reactions, rxn_006 and rxn_008, are not conservation failures but should be written with 2 H2O if explicit coefficients are given. The strategy is well aligned with the literature because it uses aminoacetonitrile and glycine imine as the main hubs and explicitly recognizes HCN concentration, glyoxylate supply, hydroxy-acid competition, and glycine thermolability as bottlenecks. The strongest routes are P5 and P10. Confidence stays below network approval because environmental annotation is often too generic or mislabeled, especially the repeated Biochemical setting, the cyanosulfidic branch omits key UV, sulfide, copper, phosphate, and staging details in rxn_002, the Fe branch is less well specified in rxn_010 than the experimental Fe(II), Fe0, green-rust, or brucite literature, and the hydantoin branch needs clearer pH and temperature conditions. Adding a glycolonitrile-to-glycolate competition step and explicit quench and transfer justifications would improve the network.\n- Issues: ['This pathway matches cyanosulfidic logic, but rxn_002 omits the experimentally important UV, H2S, Cu photoredox, phosphate, and staged surface-pool conditions. Without those details the step is only partially specified. The downstream chemistry is plausible, but the environmental support is weaker than for the direct Strecker routes.', 'This is the most condition-sensitive surface pathway in the network. The cyanosulfidic entry step is underspecified, and the hydantoin segment also needs clearer CO2 trapping and hydrolysis conditions. The chemistry is not impossible, but too many key experimental details are compressed into generic reaction labels.', 'Chemically possible, but this is the most compressed hydrothermal variant in the network. Moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation, rapid quench, CO2 trapping, hydantoin formation, and hydantoin hydrolysis likely require distinct conditions that are not sufficiently described. The route is plausible in principle but underspecified in practice.', 'The glyoxylate-to-glycine-imine step is well grounded, but the Fe route is weaker as drawn because rxn_010 uses H2 as the reductant on an Fe mineral surface. The cited literature more directly supports Fe(II), Fe0, mixed-valence iron hydroxides, green rust, or ferroan brucite mediated reduction rather than simple H2 hydrogenation on Fe mineral. The pathway concept is good, but the reduction step is not ideally specified.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network uses the two experimentally strongest glycine-forming hubs without privileging either framework. The cyanide branch takes HCN, formaldehyde, and ammonia to aminoacetonitrile by direct surface Strecker chemistry, by a glycolonitrile entry branch under staged surface conditions, or by moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation; aminoacetonitrile then reaches glycine by one-pot hydrolysis, stepwise hydrolysis via glycinamide, or CO2 capture to hydantoin followed by hydrolysis. In parallel, a non-cyanide branch forms glycine imine from glyoxylate plus ammonia and reduces it on Ni or meteoritic metal with H2. A competing glycolate branch is included to encode the known amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid split. In line with the literature, the main unresolved bottlenecks are concentrated HCN supply and abiotic glyoxylate supply, not the final glycine-forming transformations themselves.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrocyanic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoethanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-iminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"molecular hydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct surface Strecker condensation to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline aqueous pools, pH about 8-9; NH3 comparable to HCN; evaporative concentration or local reducing volcanic/plume inputs needed to sustain cyanide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation from formaldehyde and ammonia followed by cyanide addition; shown here as the overall net Strecker step to the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aqueous Strecker chemistry is experimentally robust and needs no catalyst once HCN, formaldehyde, and NH3 coexist; Miller-type discharge products and NH3-rich carbonaceous parent bodies are both consistent with this framework (Johnson et al., 2008; Glavin et al., 2025). The major uncertainty is not this step itself but generating concentrated HCN under realistic local atmospheric or surface conditions, especially for neutral CO2-N2 atmospheres (Cleaves et al., 2008; Kuwahara et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation to glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Evaporating or intermittently wetted pools where cyanide can accumulate; no dedicated mineral catalyst required for the net cyanohydrin formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic cyanide addition to formaldehyde gives the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is the direct cyanohydrin of formaldehyde and an experimentally important branch point toward glycine in cyanide systems (Patel et al., 2015). Its buildup is chemically straightforward but still inherits the general geochemical problem of producing and concentrating HCN in surface settings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile ammonolysis to aminoacetonitrile under staged surface conditions\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with H2S present as reductant; staged surface-pool or stream chemistry; phosphate buffers and complexes copper.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia converts the cyanohydrin branch toward the aminonitrile; the net elemental change is glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile with water as by-product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) route glycine precursors through glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile in a UV-driven H2S/Cu/phosphate cyanosulfidic network. This node records the net carbon and nitrogen transfer while putting the experimentally important photoredox staging into the conditions. The chemistry is supported, but the need for sequential cyanide-rich surface settings remains geochemically contested (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"CO2/H2 gas phase; subseafloor or vent-wall porewater; rapid quench below about 370K after formation to preserve products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker condensation under redox-buffered conditions; aminoacetonitrile is used as the mechanistic aminonitrile intermediate leading to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker experiments at 150 C and 10 bar generated glycine as the dominant amino acid; aminoacetonitrile is the accepted mechanistic intermediate, so it is used here as the hydrothermal hub (Hennet et al., 1992; Andersson and Holm, 2000). This branch is deliberately restricted to moderate hydrothermal conditions and rapid quench because prolonged residence above about 240 C destroys amino acids (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to mild confinement\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Prebiotic pools or cooled vent-effluent interfaces; net hydrolysis with 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; aided by pH or geothermal cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis collapsed into a single net step from aminoacetonitrile to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolyzes through glycinamide to glycine in aqueous Strecker chemistry; this node is the mass-balanced one-pot net version of that accepted sequence, with two waters implicit (Johnson et al., 2008). Placing the hydrolysis in cooler pools or interfaces rather than hot vents follows the thermal-stability critique of amino acid accumulation at high temperature (Bada et al., 1995).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to mild confinement\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous pools or interfaces; first hydrolytic step of the Strecker nitrile sequence.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydration of the nitrile function gives the corresponding amide, glycinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycinamide is the first hydrolysis product in the standard aminoacetonitrile-to-glycine sequence and is part of the accepted mechanistic picture of aqueous Strecker chemistry (Johnson et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glycinamide to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to mild confinement\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous cycling, including geothermal heating/cooling or wet-dry concentration phases.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis liberates glycine and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Further amide hydrolysis releases glycine and ammonia; this explicit step unpacks the net hydrolysis in rxn_005 and is plausible in warm aqueous cycling regimes (Johnson et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type carboxylative cyclization to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-303K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"CO2-rich or bicarbonate-rich prebiotic pools; carbamylation and cyclization can occur before later warm hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO2 traps the aminonitrile as a carbamylated intermediate that cyclizes to hydantoin; the net stoichiometry is shown here.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"CO2-linked hydantoin formation is the key Bucherer-Bergs variant connecting cyanide chemistry to amino acid release; hydantoin formation at ambient temperature followed by later hydrolysis was demonstrated for prebiotic amino acid synthesis (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). Kaur et al. (2024) highlight this branch as a bridge between cyanide and ketoacid frameworks, though it remains cyanide-dependent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous hydrolysis; 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; compatible with acid/base or geothermal cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening, hydrolysis, and decarboxylative release convert hydantoin to glycine, NH3, and CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Warm aqueous hydrolysis of hydantoin releases glycine, NH3, and CO2 in the Bucherer-Bergs sequence, and hydantoin-containing HCN hydrolysates are also consistent with glycine release (Pulletikurti et al., 2022; Ferris et al., 1974).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Condensation of glyoxylate with ammonia to glycine imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1-5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Mineral interfaces or concentrated prebiotic pools, typically pH 8-10; hemiaminal-imine equilibrium precedes reduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia form a hemiaminal that dehydrates to the alpha-imino acid intermediate glycine imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Paczelt et al. (2023) directly identified glycine imine as the elusive intermediate in glyoxylate reductive amination, while Mayer and Moran (2022) showed that glyoxylate is exceptionally reactive toward nonenzymatic reductive amination. The chemistry of this condensation is therefore strong; the unresolved issue is upstream glyoxylate supply.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenative reduction of glycine imine to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\", \"meteoritic Fe/Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or meteoritic Fe/Ni metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8; H2-rich mineral interfaces; no reaction in the cited study without a metal catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Adsorbed imine hydrogenation on metal surfaces gives the corresponding amine, glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Low-temperature H2-driven reduction of glyoxylate-derived imines to glycine is strongly supported on Ni catalysts and ground iron meteorites, with useful yields even at low-mM ammonium (Kaur et al., 2024). Fe-based variants also exist (Muchowska et al., 2019; Barge et al., 2020; Chimiak et al., 2024), but this node uses the most explicit H2-backed implementation for clean mass balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrolysis of glycolonitrile to glycolate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"NH3-poor or HCN-rich pools; 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; competing branch to the aminonitrile route.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic conversion of the cyanohydrin nitrile gives the hydroxy-acid branch product glycolate and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This branch encodes the experimentally important amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid split: when glycolonitrile hydrolyzes rather than undergoing ammonolysis, glycolate is formed instead of aminoacetonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). Including this competition reflects the known sensitivity of product ratios to pH and the NH3/HCN ratio highlighted in the review.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker with one-pot hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct surface Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile followed by full aqueous hydrolysis to glycine in cooler prebiotic pools or interfaces.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker via glycinamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct surface Strecker entry followed by stepwise hydrolysis through the explicitly represented amide intermediate glycinamide.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker via hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct surface Strecker entry, CO2 capture of the aminonitrile as hydantoin, then hydrolytic release of glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile entry with one-pot hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde first enters as glycolonitrile, then ammonia gives aminoacetonitrile under staged surface conditions, followed by one-pot hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile entry via glycinamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Staged glycolonitrile entry to aminoacetonitrile followed by explicit amide formation and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile entry via hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"Staged glycolonitrile entry followed by CO2 trapping as hydantoin and hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker with quench and one-pot hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Aminoacetonitrile is generated in moderate hydrothermal conditions, then quenched into cooler aqueous settings where it hydrolyzes to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker with quench via glycinamide\",\n      \"description\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker entry followed by stepwise post-quench hydrolysis through glycinamide.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker with quench via hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"Moderate hydrothermal Strecker entry followed by post-quench CO2 capture as hydantoin and hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate reductive amination via glycine imine\",\n      \"description\": \"Non-cyanide mineral-interface route in which glyoxylate forms glycine imine and is then hydrogenated on Ni or meteoritic metal; chemically strong, but upstream glyoxylate supply remains the main uncertainty.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_014\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "This network uses the two experimentally strongest glycine-forming hubs without privileging either framework. The cyanide branch takes HCN, formaldehyde, and ammonia to aminoacetonitrile by direct surface Strecker chemistry, by a glycolonitrile entry branch under staged surface conditions, or by moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation; aminoacetonitrile then reaches glycine by one-pot hydrolysis, stepwise hydrolysis via glycinamide, or CO2 capture to hydantoin followed by hydrolysis. In parallel, a non-cyanide branch forms glycine imine from glyoxylate plus ammonia and reduces it on Ni or meteoritic metal with H2. A competing glycolate branch is included to encode the known amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid split. In line with the literature, the main unresolved bottlenecks are concentrated HCN supply and abiotic glyoxylate supply, not the final glycine-forming transformations themselves.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hyd