S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetic acid", "common_name": "glycolate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Direct surface Strecker condensation to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Mildly alkaline aqueous pools, pH about 8-9; NH3 comparable to HCN; evaporative concentration or local reducing volcanic/plume inputs needed to sustain cyanide."}, "mechanism": "Imine formation from formaldehyde and ammonia followed by cyanide addition; shown here as the overall net Strecker step to the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Aqueous Strecker chemistry is experimentally robust and needs no catalyst once HCN, formaldehyde, and NH3 coexist; Miller-type discharge products and NH3-rich carbonaceous parent bodies are both consistent with this framework (Johnson et al., 2008; Glavin et al., 2025). The major uncertainty is not this step itself but generating concentrated HCN under realistic local atmospheric or surface conditions, especially for neutral CO2-N2 atmospheres (Cleaves et al., 2008; Kuwahara et al., 2012)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation to glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Evaporating or intermittently wetted pools where cyanide can accumulate; no dedicated mineral catalyst required for the net cyanohydrin formation."}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic cyanide addition to formaldehyde gives the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile is the direct cyanohydrin of formaldehyde and an experimentally important branch point toward glycine in cyanide systems (Patel et al., 2015). Its buildup is chemically straightforward but still inherits the general geochemical problem of producing and concentrating HCN in surface settings."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Glycolonitrile ammonolysis to aminoacetonitrile under staged surface conditions", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox", "phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox", "aqueous": "true", "other": "UV irradiation with H2S present as reductant; staged surface-pool or stream chemistry; phosphate buffers and complexes copper."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia converts the cyanohydrin branch toward the aminonitrile; the net elemental change is glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile with water as by-product.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) route glycine precursors through glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile in a UV-driven H2S/Cu/phosphate cyanosulfidic network. This node records the net carbon and nitrogen transfer while putting the experimentally important photoredox staging into the conditions. The chemistry is supported, but the need for sequential cyanide-rich surface settings remains geochemically contested (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Moderate hydrothermal Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "420-430K", "pressure": "10 bar", "catalyst": "pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox buffer", "aqueous": "true", "other": "CO2/H2 gas phase; subseafloor or vent-wall porewater; rapid quench below about 370K after formation to preserve products."}, "mechanism": "Hydrothermal Strecker condensation under redox-buffered conditions; aminoacetonitrile is used as the mechanistic aminonitrile intermediate leading to glycine.", "reasoning": "Moderate hydrothermal Strecker experiments at 150 C and 10 bar generated glycine as the dominant amino acid; aminoacetonitrile is the accepted mechanistic intermediate, so it is used here as the hydrothermal hub (Hennet et al., 1992; Andersson and Holm, 2000). This branch is deliberately restricted to moderate hydrothermal conditions and rapid quench because prolonged residence above about 240 C destroys amino acids (Bada et al., 1995; Aubrey et al., 2009)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "One-pot hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm to mild confinement", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Prebiotic pools or cooled vent-effluent interfaces; net hydrolysis with 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; aided by pH or geothermal cycling."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis collapsed into a single net step from aminoacetonitrile to glycine.", "reasoning": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolyzes through glycinamide to glycine in aqueous Strecker chemistry; this node is the mass-balanced one-pot net version of that accepted sequence, with two waters implicit (Johnson et al., 2008). Placing the hydrolysis in cooler pools or interfaces rather than hot vents follows the thermal-stability critique of amino acid accumulation at high temperature (Bada et al., 1995)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm to mild confinement", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Warm aqueous pools or interfaces; first hydrolytic step of the Strecker nitrile sequence."}, "mechanism": "Hydration of the nitrile function gives the corresponding amide, glycinamide.", "reasoning": "Glycinamide is the first hydrolysis product in the standard aminoacetonitrile-to-glycine sequence and is part of the accepted mechanistic picture of aqueous Strecker chemistry (Johnson et al., 2008)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of glycinamide to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm to mild confinement", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Warm aqueous cycling, including geothermal heating/cooling or wet-dry concentration phases."}, "mechanism": "Amide hydrolysis liberates glycine and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Further amide hydrolysis releases glycine and ammonia; this explicit step unpacks the net hydrolysis in rxn_005 and is plausible in warm aqueous cycling regimes (Johnson et al., 2008)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type carboxylative cyclization to hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "290-303K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "CO2-rich or bicarbonate-rich prebiotic pools; carbamylation and cyclization can occur before later warm hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "CO2 traps the aminonitrile as a carbamylated intermediate that cyclizes to hydantoin; the net stoichiometry is shown here.", "reasoning": "CO2-linked hydantoin formation is the key Bucherer-Bergs variant connecting cyanide chemistry to amino acid release; hydantoin formation at ambient temperature followed by later hydrolysis was demonstrated for prebiotic amino acid synthesis (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). Kaur et al. (2024) highlight this branch as a bridge between cyanide and ketoacid frameworks, though it remains cyanide-dependent."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "340-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Warm aqueous hydrolysis; 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; compatible with acid/base or geothermal cycling."}, "mechanism": "Ring opening, hydrolysis, and decarboxylative release convert hydantoin to glycine, NH3, and CO2.", "reasoning": "Warm aqueous hydrolysis of hydantoin releases glycine, NH3, and CO2 in the Bucherer-Bergs sequence, and hydantoin-containing HCN hydrolysates are also consistent with glycine release (Pulletikurti et al., 2022; Ferris et al., 1974)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Condensation of glyoxylate with ammonia to glycine imine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-343K", "pressure": "1-5 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Mineral interfaces or concentrated prebiotic pools, typically pH 8-10; hemiaminal-imine equilibrium precedes reduction."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate and ammonia form a hemiaminal that dehydrates to the alpha-imino acid intermediate glycine imine.", "reasoning": "Paczelt et al. (2023) directly identified glycine imine as the elusive intermediate in glyoxylate reductive amination, while Mayer and Moran (2022) showed that glyoxylate is exceptionally reactive toward nonenzymatic reductive amination. The chemistry of this condensation is therefore strong; the unresolved issue is upstream glyoxylate supply."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrogenative reduction of glycine imine to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "meteoritic Fe/Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or meteoritic Fe/Ni metal", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 8; H2-rich mineral interfaces; no reaction in the cited study without a metal catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Adsorbed imine hydrogenation on metal surfaces gives the corresponding amine, glycine.", "reasoning": "Low-temperature H2-driven reduction of glyoxylate-derived imines to glycine is strongly supported on Ni catalysts and ground iron meteorites, with useful yields even at low-mM ammonium (Kaur et al., 2024). Fe-based variants also exist (Muchowska et al., 2019; Barge et al., 2020; Chimiak et al., 2024), but this node uses the most explicit H2-backed implementation for clean mass balance."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Competing hydrolysis of glycolonitrile to glycolate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "NH3-poor or HCN-rich pools; 2 H2O implicit in stoichiometry; competing branch to the aminonitrile route."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic conversion of the cyanohydrin nitrile gives the hydroxy-acid branch product glycolate and releases ammonia.", "reasoning": "This branch encodes the experimentally important amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid split: when glycolonitrile hydrolyzes rather than undergoing ammonolysis, glycolate is formed instead of aminoacetonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). Including this competition reflects the known sensitivity of product ratios to pH and the NH3/HCN ratio highlighted in the review."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Surface Strecker with one-pot hydrolysis", "description": "Direct surface Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile followed by full aqueous hydrolysis to glycine in cooler p